Four approval fixtures, five trust rows, and a --reset-approvals affordance. Flag parsing is read from process.argv (:470-478 already gates the CLI entry) exactly as the module's existing isMain does.

APPROVAL FIXTURES, four rows with NO hand-written nonce, so decisionNonce is the only thing that produces a nonce string anywhere in the repository. Every delta_micros is INSIDE its class micro-limit, because the gate applies to the approval path too and a fixture above the limit would make its own criterion unrunnable. All four carry impact, downside, evidence_refs and expires_at; the first three expire 24 hours from the moment of writing and the fourth expires 72 hours in the past, which is what makes it lapsed from the first second of a fresh seed and not merely expired later. Their ids carry no prefix of their own beyond the apv_seed_ the fixtures are named by, so decisionNonce('apv_seed_budget_1') is 'apr_apv_seed_budget_1' and the readable string is never written by hand.
  {approval_id:'apv_seed_budget_1', action_class:'budget-change', action:'update_campaign_budget', resource:'adset_001', constraints:{delta_micros:40_000_000}, impact:'raise brand-defence daily budget by ₹40', downside:'spend on saturated brand terms lifts qualified CPL', evidence_refs:['evt_seed_journey_spend_1','evt_seed_journey_lead_qualified_1']}
  {approval_id:'apv_seed_campaign_1', action_class:'campaign-status', action:'set_campaign_status', resource:'campaign_001', constraints:{status:'PAUSED'}, impact:'pause brand defence while the new creative is built', downside:'brand coverage gap for one day', evidence_refs:['evt_seed_journey_lead_qualified_2']}
  {approval_id:'apv_seed_geography_1', action_class:'new-geography', action:'create_campaign', resource:'campaign_004', constraints:{name:'Retargeting — new region'}, impact:'open a new region for retargeting', downside:'new spend on untested geography', evidence_refs:['evt_seed_journey_spend_1']}. resource here is the PROPOSED campaign id and is deliberately not a fixture in the fake's FIXTURES (which hold campaign_001/002/003); the executor reads the name from constraints.name for create_campaign, so the created campaign is called 'Retargeting — new region' and not 'campaign_004'.
  {approval_id:'apv_seed_lapsed_1', action_class:'budget-change', action:'update_campaign_budget', resource:'adset_002', constraints:{delta_micros:30_000_000}, impact:'raise prospecting budget', downside:'prospecting CPL drifts above target', evidence_refs:[], expires_at: now - 72h}
  So on seed data the page renders EXACTLY THREE pending cards, one lapsed row, and nothing in the executed section until QA decides something.

TRUST ROWS, five, written through repositories.trustLedger.upsert: campaign-status 6/6/0/0 (autonomous), budget-change 3/2/1/0 (approval required: precision 0.67 is below the 0.90 threshold), creative-refresh 4/4/0/0 pinned=true (shadow), new-geography 2/2/0/0 pinned=true (shadow, and the policy-only class), and NO ROW AT ALL for campaign-launch - the one no-evidence fail-closed class, and the only shadow rows are the two pinned ones. These numbers are calibration fixtures chosen by this plan; what must not happen is the table and the gate reading different data, which is why both read trust.postureFor over these rows and a test asserts the rendered table against the gate's own verdict.

--reset-approvals, parsed from process.argv, does five things in this order and prints a summary naming each count so QA can tell a lock from an empty queue: (1) repositories.actionRecords.purgeSeeded(TENANT.id, SEEDED_APPROVAL_IDS.map(decisionNonce)); (2) repositories.idempotency.release(TENANT.id, decisionNonce(id), 'executor') for each of the four ids - release, the real method on the flat idempotency repository (:133-172), taking exactly the three arguments claim took, and not a removeByNonces or removeByPrefix that does not exist on it; (3) the approvals reset, WHICH STAMPS THE TWO GROUPS DIFFERENTLY AND THIS IS THE POINT: repositories.approvals.resetToSeed with expires_at = now + 24h for the THREE ids SEEDED_PENDING_IDS (budget, campaign, geography), and resetToSeed with expires_at = now - 72h for apv_seed_lapsed_1. Re-stamping all four to now + 24h would make the lapsed fixture pending again - resetToSeed returns every seeded row to status 'pending' - so the queue would render FOUR pending cards and ZERO lapsed rows, contradicting the seed's own defining property, the page test's count, and qa_script steps 2 and 12. The lapsed fixture's stamp is its identity, so the reset restores it, and the summary prints the pending and lapsed counts SEPARATELY ('3 approvals pending, 1 lapsed') so QA reads four rows restored without reading '4' as '4 pending'. raw_events is NOT re-stamped and repositories.derived is NOT touched: raw_events is append-only with both triggers and re-dating it is not possible, so the maturity the gate reads is whatever the last seed stamped. (4) guardian.resetSeededKillSwitches(TENANT.id), clearing exactly scope 'provider' / scope_id 'meta_ads' - the row freeze's own defaults write and activeFor now reads. (5) nothing else. The nonce list is built by CALLING decisionNonce over the same SEEDED_APPROVAL_IDS the fixtures use, so a future rename of a fixture cannot make the reset miss.

The plain `npm run sdlc:seed` path gains the four approvals and five trust rows alongside everything it already writes, and the existing alreadySeeded summary gains their counts.

review.detail:
Both guardian routes call guardian.freeze / guardian.reEnable, which THROW a typed error (FREEZE_SCOPE_UNKNOWN) for an unrecognised scope. Neither route catches, so an invalid scope produces an opaque 500 {code:'INTERNAL'} instead of the 400 + typed code the guardian module already defines. The write fails closed — the throw precedes the kill-switch upsert — so this is a lost-diagnostic problem, not a safety one.

`src/api/routes.js:851`

Evidence: POST /v1/guardian/trigger?kind=spend-spike with body {"scope":"galaxy","actor":"x"} ->
  HTTP 500 {"code":"INTERNAL","message":"internal error"}

POST /v1/guardian/re-enable with body {"actor":"owner","scope":"galaxy"} ->
  HTTP 500 {"code":"INTERNAL","message":"internal error"}

By contrast the sibling guard on the same route, the missing actor, answers properly:
  POST /v1/guardian/re-enable with {} -> HTTP 400 {"code":"RE_ENABLE_ACTOR_REQUIRED",...}

FREEZE_SCOPE_UNKNOWN is defined and carries .code/.details (src/strategy/guardian.js:161-169, :210-212) but no route ever surfaces it.

Fix: Validate the scope in the route (or wrap the guardian call) and return errorResponse(response, 400, {code: error.code, message: error.message, details: error.details}), so FREEZE_SCOPE_UNKNOWN reaches the caller the way RE_ENABLE_ACTOR_REQUIRED already does.
